@h1 Parameters & the invocation forms h1@

A parameter is @strong metadata strong@ that rides alongside a node's content —
a link's address, a card's status, a figure's caption. Wit gives you five
syntaxes for passing parameters, and they all bind to the same names. The
guiding principle is @strong the form you open with is the form that closes
strong@.

A single definition — here a card that names a title and a status — can be
filled every way, and every buildable form renders identically.

@@@pre
#card ||title, status||
Title ::title:: / Status ::status::
card#
pre@@@

@@@pre
@card { title - Alpha, status - draft }       ~ record-arg (self-closing)
@card { title: Alpha, status: draft }         ~ record-arg, colon separator
@card title:Alpha status:draft card@          ~ colon-scatter (one-line body)
@card                                          ~ form-fill: the body IS the params
  title: Alpha
  status: draft
card@
@card(title Alpha, status draft)              ~ parens (self-closing)
@card(title: Alpha, status: draft)            ~ parens, colon separator
@card |title Alpha| |status draft| card@      ~ pipes (carries a body)
pre@@@

All render @code Title Alpha / Status draft code@. (The fixture folder
26-all-param-forms collects these; its combined showcase file intentionally
fails to build, so lean on the individual forms.)

@h2 Choosing a form h2@

@ul
@li @strong Structured, no body? strong@ Prefer @strong record-arg strong@ with
a colon — @@@code @card { title: X } code@@@. li@
@li @strong Tag a node mid-sentence? strong@ @strong Colon-scatter strong@ —
@@@code @figure src:lamp caption:"the lens" figure@ code@@@. li@
@li @strong Many fields, or a multi-line value? strong@ Use a @strong form-fill
strong@ body. li@
@li @strong One or two short params inline? strong@ Reach for @strong parens
strong@ — @@@code @badge(tone good) code@@@. li@
@li @strong Need a body, or switching a value mid-body? strong@ Only @strong
pipes strong@ compose with body content — but they are the noisiest form and the
one prone to greedy-bind, so avoid them otherwise. li@
ul@

@h2 The shared slot grammar h2@

Record-arg, colon-scatter, parens, and pipes all read each slot with the same
grammar:

@ul
@li @strong Positional strong@ — a single token with no space: @@@code |full|
code@@@ binds a value with no name. It is a @em value em@, not a flag. li@
@li @strong Named strong@ — @code key value code@: the first word is the key,
the rest is the value, which may be several words. @@@code |mood calm| code@@@
sets @code mood code@ to @code calm code@. li@
@li @strong Named, multi-word key strong@ — join words with a spaced hyphen
@code  -  code@: @@@code |background colour - dark slate| code@@@ has key
@code background colour code@. An embedded hyphen with no surrounding spaces
stays in the value — @@@code |well-known| code@@@. li@
@li @strong Flag strong@ — a trailing @code ! code@ marks a boolean-style flag
with an empty value: @@@code |full width!| code@@@. A @code ! code@ in the
middle of a value is just punctuation. li@
ul@

A value that is exactly @code "quoted" code@ is unquoted, with @code \" code@
and @code \\ code@ recognised. Every non-flag value is then @strong type-probed
strong@ — run through the scalar classifier — so a number, record, or
collection passed through @em any em@ form becomes a real typed value you can
iterate, compare, or reach into. See
@a |href /docs/guides/data/| Type-classified scalars a@.

@h2 Record-arg h2@

A record literal placed right after a handle — @@@code @name { field - value, … }
code@@@ — whose fields become named parameters. It is @strong self-closing
strong@ (the matching @@@code } code@@@ ends the node) and the @strong exclusive
strong@ form: it will not share a node with any other parameter source. Reach
for it when the arguments read like structured data.

@@@pre
@card { title - Alpha, status - draft }
pre@@@

Both fields bind to the card's captures and render
@code Title Alpha / Status draft code@ (verified). The separator can be a spaced
hyphen or a colon:

@@@pre
@card { title: Epsilon, status: draft }
pre@@@

The brace binds to explicit captures (template-expansion) and to captures
inferred from the body (template-implicit-captures) alike (fixture
22-record-args/inline-single-field, inline-multi-field).

The record grammar is identical inline and across lines, so a many-field call
can breathe — fields separate on a comma @strong or strong@ a newline, and
emphasis markers inside a value stay literal:

@@@pre
@reference_entry {
  author - Ursula Franklin
  title - The Real World of Technology
  year - 1989
}
pre@@@

(fixture 22-record-args/multi-line-record.)

@h3 The comma-in-value trap h3@

An @strong unquoted comma splits fields strong@. Because the parser reads a
comma as a field boundary, a value that contains one is misread as a new field
with no key:

@@@pre
@cite { author - Boud, D., year - 2024 }   ~ E_MALFORMED_RECORD
pre@@@

That is @code E_MALFORMED_RECORD code@ — "record field missing key" (verified).
Quote the value to protect the comma, which also forces a string type:

@@@pre
@cite { author: "Boud, D.", year: 2024 }   ~ renders: Boud, D. (2024)
pre@@@

(Same root cause as fixture 09-records/comma-in-value.)

@h2 Colon-scatter h2@

In a @strong single-line strong@ body, tokens shaped @code id:value code@ with
@strong zero whitespace strong@ are lifted out as parameters; the rest of the
line stays prose. It is the lightest way to tag a node mid-sentence:

@@@pre
@figure src:lamp caption:"Second-order Fresnel" figure@
pre@@@

@code src code@ and @code caption code@ become params; anything else on the line
is the figure's body.

@h3 The strict contract h3@

Scatter fires only under an exact contract, and that strictness keeps ordinary
prose from being mis-read:

@ul
@li The id is @code [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]* code@. li@
@li The value is a bare word @code [A-Za-z0-9_-]+ code@, a @code "quoted" code@
string (spaces allowed), an @code _italic_ code@ or @code *bold* code@ run, or
any @@@code @node code@@@ (bare, parens, or with a closer). li@
@li There is @strong no whitespace anywhere strong@ — including between the
@code : code@ and the value. li@
@li The byte just before the id must be the start of the line or a non-word,
non-backslash character. li@
ul@

@@@code @thing name:Tauraj thing@ code@@@ lifts @code name code@ to
@code Tauraj code@ (fixture 24-colon-scatter/body-scatter-single); a quoted value
carries spaces — @@@code @thing mode:"complex value" thing@ code@@@
(body-scatter-quoted). Multiple tokens lift while residual prose remains
(body-scatter-multi), and a repeated key is last-one-wins
(body-scatter-override).

Because the value may itself be a node or an emphasis run, scatter lifts them as
sibling content: italic (body-scatter-italic-value), bold
(body-scatter-bold-value), a closer-form node (body-scatter-node-value), a
self-closing parens node (body-scatter-self-closing-node-value), a node carrying
its own params (body-scatter-multiple-node-params), a node then a bare token
(body-scatter-node-then-bare), and overriding a value with a node
(body-scatter-override-with-node).

@h3 A space breaks the contract h3@

Add a space after the colon and the token is prose again — @@@code @thing key:
value thing@ code@@@ lifts nothing (fixture
24-colon-scatter/body-scatter-space-after-colon-is-prose, verified parameter
source "none"). A real inline node on that line stays a node; only the
scatter-lift is suppressed.

To keep a literal @code id:value code@ in prose, escape the colon —
@code name\:Tauraj code@ suppresses the lift and the backslash is stripped from
the output (body-scatter-escape).

@aside(class note) 💡 The strict contract has one honest downside — a space-free @code id:value code@ buried in real prose still lifts. @@@code @thing the variable name:Tauraj is bad thing@ code@@@ lifts @code name code@ even though you meant prose (fixture false-positive-prose, verified). Escape the colon or add a space. The same pattern bites URLs, ratios, and times written mid-sentence. aside@

@h2 Form-fill body h2@

When a node's body reads like a form — every line a @code key: value code@ pair
— the body @strong becomes strong@ the parameters. It is the most readable form
for many-field templates (citations, cards, front-matter), and the only call
form that can carry @strong multi-line strong@ values.

@@@pre
@cite
  author: Smith
  year: 2024
cite@
pre@@@

This binds @code author code@ and @code year code@; @code year code@ is
type-probed to the number @code 2024 code@ (fixture
23-form-fill/template-invocation).

@h3 What triggers form-fill h3@

A body is read as form-fill when @strong all three strong@ hold: it contains a
newline; its first non-blank, non-comment line matches @code <id>: code@; and it
has @strong at least two content lines strong@. A @strong single strong@ content
line is @em not em@ form-fill — it falls through to colon-scatter. That two-line
threshold is the rule most worth remembering.

@h3 The line grammar h3@

Each line is @code <id>: <value> code@. One optional leading space after the
colon is trimmed, trailing whitespace is trimmed, and @code ~ code@ comments and
blank lines are ignored (fixture 23-form-fill/with-comments). A line that does
not match the shape is @code E_MALFORMED_FORM_FIELD code@.

A quoted value protects commas and forces a string type. And — one surprise
worth internalising — emphasis markers in a value stay @strong literal strong@,
because the inline parser is bypassed: @@@code _title_ code@@@ renders as the
three characters, not italic (fixtures with-quoted-string,
with-emphasis-in-value, value-emphasis-renders).

@h3 Multi-line values h3@

Give a key an @strong empty same-line value strong@ and then indent the content
deeper: the block is consumed, common indentation is stripped, and lines join
with newlines.

@@@pre
@note
  body:
    First line.
    Second line.
note@
pre@@@

That binds @code body code@ to @code First line. code@ + newline +
@code Second line. code@ (fixture 23-form-fill/value-multi-line). Blank lines
inside the block are kept (value-multi-paragraph); a @strong dedent ends the
value strong@ (value-block-end-by-dedent); and — a sharp edge — an indented line
that @em looks like em@ @code key: code@ is treated as @strong content, not a new
field strong@ (value-indented-key-shape-is-content). An empty value with no
indented block simply moves on to the next field (value-empty-then-next-field).

The identical block grammar appears in @strong definitions strong@ — a block
record def and a value-block def both collapse to a record:

@@@pre
#point
  x: 3
  y: 7
point#

#point:
  x: 3
  y: 7
!!
pre@@@

(fixtures 23-form-fill/block-record-def, value-block-record-def; a realistic
@@@code +#bibliography code@@@ value-block wrapping a form-fill with quoted
commas is bibliography-style.)

@h2 Parens h2@

Parens — @@@code @name(p, q) code@@@ — are the compact, @strong self-closing
strong@ form: the closing @code ) code@ ends the node, so there is no
@@@code name@ code@@@ closer to write. Params are comma-separated, and colon or
space separates each key from its value. Use parens for one or two short
parameters and no body — @@@code @badge(tone good) code@@@,
@@@code @figure(src lamp.png, caption The lens) code@@@.

@@@pre
@badge(tone good)
The keeper logged the lamp at dawn.
pre@@@

The badge self-closes at @code ) code@; the sentence below is its own paragraph
(fixture 05-nodes-parens/self-closing). The key/value separator can be a space
or a colon interchangeably — @@@code @scene(mood calm, location harbour) code@@@
and @@@code @scene(mood: calm, location: harbour) code@@@ bind identically
(multiple-params, colon-separator). A multi-word @strong key strong@ joins with a
spaced hyphen — @@@code @panel(background colour - dark slate) code@@@
(hyphen-multi-word-key).

@ul
@li @strong Inner whitespace is trimmed strong@ — @@@code @x( a , b ) code@@@
binds @code a code@ and @code b code@ (inner-whitespace). li@
@li @strong A trailing comma is tolerated strong@ — @@@code @x(a,) code@@@
(trailing-comma). li@
@li @strong Empty parens are legal strong@ and @em distinct em@ from a bare
reference — @@@code @x() code@@@ carries a parens source with no slots, while
@@@code @x code@@@ carries none (empty-parens). li@
ul@

@h3 Parens carries no body of its own h3@

If you follow a parens call with body content and a closer, a @strong core node
strong@ still attaches the params and renders the body —
@@@code @aside(tone wry) the keeper said nothing aside@ code@@@ works. But a
@strong template definition strong@ with no body slot has nowhere to put a
following body, so it is silently dropped (fixture
05-nodes-parens/parens-then-body). @strong For a body, use pipes strong@ —
@@@code @aside |tone wry| the keeper said nothing aside@ code@@@.

A parens call must also stay on @strong one line strong@. Wrapping it across a
newline breaks it — the remainder, including the @code ) code@, leaks into the
surrounding text (multi-line-call). A value long enough to want wrapping is a
sign to use form-fill or record-arg.

@h2 Pipes h2@

Pipes — @@@code @name |slot| code@@@ — are the @strong only strong@ form that
composes with a body. A slot can sit on the opening line or scatter through the
body, and duplicate keys resolve last-one-wins. Reach for pipes when a node has
both parameters and content, or to switch a value partway through — otherwise
prefer record-arg or colon-scatter.

@@@pre
@figure |src lamp.png| |full width!| |caption The second-order lens| figure@
pre@@@

That passes a named @code src code@, a @code full width code@ flag, and a
multi-word @code caption code@ (fixtures 06-parameters-pipes/basic-named,
flag-with-bang, multi-word-value).

Pipes may appear on the opening line, or anywhere inside the body. When a key
repeats, @strong the last one wins strong@ — which makes pipes the form for
switching a value mid-body:

@@@pre
@scene |mood calm|
The harbour was still.
|mood tense|
Then the bell rang.
scene@
pre@@@

Here @code mood code@ ends up @code tense code@ (fixture last-one-wins; see also
mid-body-scatter). You can also put several pipes on one line —
@@@code @x |a one| |b two| |c three| x@ code@@@ (multiple-pipes-per-line).

An empty @@@code || code@@@ inside a body contributes no parameters (empty-pipe),
and ordinary prose that merely contains pipe characters stays literal — @code The
signal flag read red | white | red. code@ renders as text (pipe-in-body-text).

@h3 The multi-line-value bug h3@

A newline @strong inside strong@ a single @@@code |…| code@@@ value does not span
lines — the pipe never closes and you get @code E_UNCLOSED_NODE code@ (fixture
multi-line-value). Keep each pipe value on one line; for a genuinely multi-line
value, use a form-fill body or a record-arg brace.

@h2 The mixing rule h2@

Two forms on one node behave differently depending on which two.

@ul
@li @strong Parens and pipes MERGE. strong@ @@@code @card(title Alpha) |status
draft| card@ code@@@ binds @em both em@ — verified. This is not an error. li@
@li @strong Record-arg is exclusive. strong@ Combining @@@code {…} code@@@ with
parens or pipes collected @em before em@ it is a hard error,
@code E_MIXED_PARAM_SOURCE code@ — verified for both @@@code @card |status Y| {
title X } code@@@ and @@@code @card(status Y) { title X } code@@@. li@
@li @strong Record-arg then pipes drops the pipes. strong@ @@@code @card { title:
X } |status Y| card@ code@@@ does not merge either: the record self-closes at
@@@code } code@@@, the trailing pipes are discarded, and the now-unfilled capture
surfaces as @code E_MISSING_RECORD_FIELD code@ — verified. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) 💡 The instinct "record-arg can't combine" is right, but the error only fires when the extra source is parsed @em before em@ the brace. A record-arg followed by more params silently drops them instead. aside@

@h2 The greedy-bind hazard h2@

A @strong bodyless pipes call strong@ — pipes with no @@@code name@ code@@@ closer
on its line — greedily swallows forward to the next matching close when nothing
separates them:

@@@pre
@card |title X|
@card |title Y| card@
pre@@@

This is @code E_UNCLOSED_NODE code@ (verified): the first call keeps reading
until it finds @@@code card@ code@@@, which belongs to the second. The fix is any
one of: give the call an explicit @@@code card@ code@@@ closer, put a @strong
blank line strong@ after it, or place a bodyless pipes call @strong last strong@.

@@@pre
@card |title X|

@card |title Y| card@
pre@@@

With the blank line, each renders on its own (verified). This is why pipes is
the noisiest form and why the other forms self-close.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Assuming record-arg merges with pipes — it errors, or drops them. li@
@li Assuming parens and pipes conflict — they merge. li@
@li Leaving a bodyless pipes call before another same-named node (greedy-bind). li@
@li Leaving an unquoted comma inside a record-arg value — quote it. li@
@li Adding a body when a parens template has no body slot — it is dropped; use pipes. li@
@li Wrapping a parens call, or a single pipe value, across lines. li@
@li Expecting a @strong one-line strong@ body to be form-fill — it is
colon-scatter (needs a space-free @code key:value code@). li@
@li Forgetting that emphasis markers are literal inside form-fill values. li@
@li An accidental @code foo:bar code@ mid-prose lifting a stray param — escape or
space it. li@
@li Writing @@@code |full| code@@@ expecting a flag — it is a positional value; a
flag needs the trailing @code ! code@. li@
ul@

